{
  "case_id": "2007-06-11543",
  "document_hash": "9c38e4433ee740636e3f604f8560261a72df768e95e81dbb7002ada56db6837d",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2007-06-11543",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c38e4433ee740636e3f604f8560261a72df768e95e81dbb7002ada56db6837d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c38e4433ee7-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:09 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-11543",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c38e4433ee740636e3f604f8560261a72df768e95e81dbb7002ada56db6837d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c38e4433ee7-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: I think you can say that all of the crimes that are listed require mens rea and DUI doesn't; does it?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2007-06-11543",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "9c38e4433ee740636e3f604f8560261a72df768e95e81dbb7002ada56db6837d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c38e4433ee7-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: May I ask this question about your interpretation of the word \"otherwise.\" Do you think it is the equivalent of the statute that omitted that word, but added in \"conduct that presents an equally serious potential risk of physical injury\"? Do you think -- in other words, do you think that the four examples define the risk of physical injury that, the risk of potential physical injury the statute"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Leondra R. Kruger",
      "case_id": "2007-06-11543",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "9c38e4433ee740636e3f604f8560261a72df768e95e81dbb7002ada56db6837d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c38e4433ee7-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. KRUGER: It has to be comparable, which is what the Court said in James --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2007-06-11543",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c38e4433ee740636e3f604f8560261a72df768e95e81dbb7002ada56db6837d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c38e4433ee7-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under 18 U.S.C. 844(i), arson is defined as maliciously damaging property by means of fire or explosives. But the way courts have interpreted that language is not to require intent to damage property in all instances. It also covers situations in which the fire is set with willful disregard for the likelihood that damage will occur; in other words, in situations in which the fire has occurred and recklessly poses harm."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2007-06-11543",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c38e4433ee740636e3f604f8560261a72df768e95e81dbb7002ada56db6837d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c38e4433ee7-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: You don't think this is just limited to intentional arson? You think negligent arson? Wow."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2007-06-11543",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c38e4433ee740636e3f604f8560261a72df768e95e81dbb7002ada56db6837d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c38e4433ee7-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What else -- what else would be in this catalogue? You rejected my speeder, but I think you said something about a reckless driver might, habitually reckless driver. What else would fit the description \"conduct that presents a serious potential risk of physical injury to another\"?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2007-06-11543",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "9c38e4433ee740636e3f604f8560261a72df768e95e81dbb7002ada56db6837d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-9c38e4433ee7-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. KATZE: The problem with the Government's interpretation is that it offers no limiting principle whatsoever. It's merely open to say absolutely any offense that would present a serious potential risk of injury to another would fall within the statute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2007-06-11543",
      "confidence": 0.0,
